UID:5e060c0d6ad4bd8c@role-tax.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260828T221753Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260827
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Killam v. MaineHealth
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE  APPELLATE DIVISON \nWORKERS’ COMPENSATION BO
 ARD  Case No. App. Div. 25-0013 \n  Decision No. 26-08 \n \n \nTAMMIE L. (
 COOMBS) KILLAM \n(Appellee) \n \nv. \n \nMAINEHEALTH \n(Appellant) \n \nan
 d \n \nSYNERNET \n(Insurer) \n \nConference held:  April 8\, 2026  \nDecid
 ed:  August 27\, 2026 \n \nPANEL MEMBERS: Administrative Law Judges Stoval
 l\, Rooks\, and Smith \nBy: Administrative Law Judge Smith \n \n[¶1]  Mai
 neHealth/Maine Coast Memorial Hospital (the Hospital) appeals \nfrom a dec
 ision of a Workers’ Compensation Board administrative law judge (Hirtle\
 , \nALJ) denying the Hospital’s Petition for Review pertaining to Tammie
  Killam’s July \n2\, 2014 \, work injury. The Hospital co ntends the ALJ
  erred by interpreting the \nindependent medical examiner’s (IME’S) op
 inion to contain an “unambiguous” \nassessment of no work capacity \, 
 and by adopting that interpretation .  We disagree \nand affirm the decisi
 on.  \nI.  BACKGROUND \n[¶2]  Tammie Killam was hired by the Hospital as 
 a full -time orderly on \nOctober 21\, 2013.  On July 2\, 2014\, while mov
 ing a table in the operating room\, she \n2 \n \nfelt and heard a pop on t
 he ulnar side of her let wrist. As established in a consent \ndecree dated
  March 7\, 2018 \, Ms. Killam sustained a work-related left wrist injury \
 non July 2\, 2014\, and resulting “psychological overlay consistent with
  an adjustment \ndisorder with depression and anxiety.” The parties agre
 ed she was entitled to receive \ntotal incapacity benefits without prejudi
 ce\, pursuant to 39-A M.R.S.A. § 212.   \n[¶3]  In 2020\, the Hospital f
 iled a Petition for Review and sought to reduce \nMs. Killam’s level of 
 benefits to reflect a  full-time\, minimum wage -earning \ncapacity. Ms. K
 illam then filed a Petition for Payment of Medical and Related \nServices.
  The ALJ found in a decree dated August 11\, 2022\, that Ms. Killam had \n
 developed causally related symptoms and limitations in her right wrist and
  remained \ntotally incapacitated to earn because of her work-related inju
 ry and its psychological \nsequela\nhttps://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/
 appellate/2026decisions/26-8_Killam_v._MaineHealth_8-27-26.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/appellate/2026decisions/26-8_Kil
 lam_v._MaineHealth_8-27-26.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
